  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 244 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\n(Thursday Night - Sunday Night)\n\nA near repeat is expected Thursday night into Friday with yet \nanother MCS likely moving just to our north. This means that exact \ntemperatures and heat index values in parts of northeast Missouri \nand west-central Illinois. Farther south however, the dangerous heat \nand humidity will continue at least through Saturday. Temperatures \nmay rise slightly with each day as the low-level thermal ridge moves \noverhead. These rising temperatures though may be offset by lower \ndewpoints as topsoil continues to dry out in many locations without \nany significant rain in 7+ days. That means that peak heat index \nvalues each day are likely to stay about the same, generally in the \n105-110F range. Due to the longevity of these values south of I-70, \ndid go ahead and convert the remainder of the advisory in southeast \nMissouri over to an extreme heat warning.\n\nConfidence decreases by Sunday as there are differences with the \ntiming of a cold front. In addition, there is a signal for another \npotential MCS late Saturday night into Sunday morning, but this \ntime, it may be farther south and impact parts of the area. This \nleaves much of the area very uncertain for specific high \ntemperatures and peak heat index values and was why heat headlines \nwere not extended through Sunday. However, the farther south you \ntravel, the better chance for another day of dangerous heat and \nhumidity. There may also be at least a low threat for a few strong \nto severe thunderstorms along/ahead of the front Sunday afternoon-\nevening given high instability and 20-25+ knots of deep-layer shear \nexpected, but exactly where is heavily dependent on the exact cold \nfrontal timing. \n\n\n(Monday - Next Wednesday)\n\nBehind the front, both temperatures and humidity levels are expected \nto come back closer to normal mid August levels. However, it is \ndefinitely a summer front where the dewpoint/humidity drop is slow. \nTherefore, a slower frontal passage than expected could mean another \nday with near 100 degree heat index values in southeast \nMissouri/southwest Illinois. By Tuesday however, heat index values \nshould stay in the 90s areawide. \n\nForecast uncertainty increases heading toward midweek (and beyond). \nClusters of the 500-hPa height pattern show the overall variability \nwell, with differences in placement and strength of the midlevel \nridge as well as a trough crossing the Great Lakes/southeast Canada. \nIf the ridge is stronger/more to the east, we may return to \ndangerous heat and humidity. However, a weaker ridge centered more \nto the southwest may allow another cold front to move through and \ncontinue the more near-normal temperatures (and bring renewed \nchances of showers and thunderstorms). The overall membership of the \nLREF is pretty split between these two scenarios, though I will note \nthat this is a bit of a change compared to yesterday, which \nsuggested a quick return to the dangerous heat and humidity.",
